-    "prompt": "# Durable request inbox: evolve the state file from v1 to v2\n\nThe `inbox` package is a small durable, multi-session request inbox.  Callers\nsubmit requests under an idempotency key, workers claim them under a\ntime-limited lease, and finally acknowledge them.  All state lives in one JSON\nfile so a fresh process resumes exactly where the previous one stopped.  Time\nalways comes from an injected clock, so nothing ever sleeps.\n\nThe package currently writes version 1 of the state file, has no notion of\npayload revisions, and contains several defects.  Evolve it to version 2.  Keep\nthe module layout (`inbox/errors.py`, `inbox/model.py`, `inbox/store.py`,\n`inbox/service.py`, `inbox/__init__.py`) and every public name that exists\ntoday.  Standard library only; no network, no subprocess, no sleeping.\n\n## 1. Errors -- `inbox.errors`\n\nSix exception types, all subclasses of `InboxError`, which subclasses\n`Exception`:\n\n* `ValidationError` -- an argument violates the contract in section 6.\n* `UnknownKeyError` -- the operation targets an idempotency key that is unknown.\n* `LeaseError` -- the entry is not claimed, or is claimed by somebody else.\n* `ConflictError` -- an acknowledgement names a stale revision.\n* `StateFormatError` -- the on-disk state file cannot be understood.\n\n## 2. Records -- `inbox.model`\n\n`STATES` is exactly the tuple `(\"pending\", \"claimed\", \"done\")`.\n\n`Entry` is a dataclass with these fields, in this order, with these defaults:\n\n| field | type | default |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `key` | `str` | required |\n| `seq` | `int` | required |\n| `payload` | `dict` | required |\n| `state` | `str` | `\"pending\"` |\n| `revision` | `int` | `1` |\n| `attempts` | `int` | `0` |\n| `created_at` | `float` | `0.0` |\n| `updated_at` | `float` | `0.0` |\n| `owner` | `str` or `None` | `None` |\n| `lease_expires_at` | `float` or `None` | `None` |\n| `result` | `dict` or `None` | `None` |\n| `acked_revision` | `int` or `None` | `None` |\n\nMethods:\n\n* `copy()` -- a new `Entry` with the same values; `payload` and `result` are\n  deep copies, so mutating either object never affects the other.\n* `lease_expired(now)` -- `True` only when `state == \"claimed\"`,\n  `lease_expires_at is not None` and `now >= lease_expires_at`.  A lease that\n  ends exactly at `now` is expired.\n* `to_dict()` -- a JSON-ready dict with exactly these twelve keys: `key`, `seq`,\n  `payload`, `state`, `revision`, `attempts`, `created_at`, `updated_at`,\n  `owner`, `lease_expires_at`, `result`, `acked_revision`.  The legacy\n  `claimed_by` key must no longer be written.  `payload` and `result` are deep\n  copies, so mutating the returned dict must not touch the entry.\n* `from_dict(raw)` -- classmethod rebuilding an entry from a v1 or a v2 record.\n  `owner` is taken from `raw[\"owner\"]` when that key is present, otherwise from\n  `raw[\"claimed_by\"]`.  Missing `payload` means `{}`; missing `revision` means\n  `1`; missing `attempts` means `0`; missing `created_at`/`updated_at` mean\n  `0.0`; missing `result`/`acked_revision`/`lease_expires_at` mean `None`.\n  Unknown keys are ignored.  It raises `StateFormatError` when `raw` is not a\n  dict, when `key` is missing or is not a string with non-blank content, when\n  `seq` is missing or is not an `int` (`bool` is not an `int` here), when\n  `state` is not in `STATES`, or when `payload` is present but is not a dict.\n\n`SubmitResult` is a frozen dataclass with fields `entry` (an `Entry`), `created`\n(`bool`) and `updated` (`bool`), in that order, plus read-only properties `key`,\n`seq`, `revision` and `state` that delegate to `entry` so that older callers\nwhich only read those attributes keep working.\n\n## 3. Storage -- `inbox.store`\n\n`CURRENT_VERSION` is `2` and `SUPPORTED_VERSIONS` is `(1, 2)`.\n\n`JsonStateStore(path)` keeps `self.path` as a `pathlib.Path` built from `path`,\nwhich may be a `str` or a `Path` and is always relative to the process.\n\n`load()` returns a `dict` mapping idempotency key to `Entry`:\n\n* A missing file yields `{}`.\n* The mapping key of `entries` is authoritative: it is used as the entry key\n  even when the record also carries a different `key` field.\n* Unknown top-level fields and unknown entry fields are ignored.\n* The stored `next_seq` is never trusted (see section 4).\n* `StateFormatError` is raised when the file is not valid JSON, when the\n  top-level value is not a JSON object, when `version` is missing or is not an\n  `int` (`bool` is not an `int` here), when `version` is not in\n  `SUPPORTED_VERSIONS`, when `entries` is missing or is not a JSON object, or\n  when any entry value is not a JSON object.\n\n`save(entries, next_seq)` writes the whole document\n`{\"version\": 2, \"next_seq\": next_seq, \"entries\": {key: entry.to_dict()}}` using\n`json.dumps(doc, sort_keys=True, indent=2)`.  It creates missing parent\ndirectories, writes to a sibling temporary file named `<filename>.tmp` and then\natomically replaces the target with `os.replace`.  A v1 file is therefore\nrewritten as v2 the first time the inbox saves.\n\n## 4. Service -- `inbox.service.Inbox`\n\n`Inbox(path, clock=time.time)`.  `clock` is a zero-argument callable returning a\nnumber of seconds; a non-callable `clock` raises `ValidationError`.  The\nconstructor loads the state file (so a damaged file raises `StateFormatError`\nhere) and never reads the clock.\n\n* `path` -- read-only property returning the store's `pathlib.Path`.\n* `next_seq` -- read-only property: the `seq` the next brand new key will get.\n  After loading it is `max(seq of loaded entries) + 1`, or `1` when there are no\n  entries, regardless of what the file said.  `seq` numbering starts at `1`.\n\nClock discipline: `submit`, `claim`, `ack`, `release` and `sweep` read the clock\nexactly once per call when their arguments are valid, and never read it when\nargument validation fails.  `get`, `list_entries` and `stats` never read it.\nEvery timestamp written is `float(clock())`.\n\nPersistence discipline: any call that changes stored state saves the whole file\nbefore returning.  A call that changes nothing -- a deduplicated submit, a claim\nthat expires nothing and claims nothing, a sweep that expires nothing, or a call\nthat raises without having expired any lease -- must not write the file at all.\n(The lease expiry that `claim`, `ack` and `release` perform is itself a change\nand is saved even when the surrounding call then raises.)\n\n### `submit(key, payload) -> SubmitResult`\n\nTwo payloads are equal iff their canonical encodings\n`json.dumps(payload, sort_keys=True, separators=(\",\", \":\"))` are equal, so key\norder never matters but `1` and `1.0` differ.  The payload is deep-copied on the\nway in; later mutation of the caller's dict must not reach the inbox.\n\n* Unknown key: create the entry with `seq = next_seq` (then bump `next_seq`),\n  `revision = 1`, `attempts = 0`, `state = \"pending\"`, `created_at` and\n  `updated_at` set to now, everything else at its default.  Returns\n  `created=True, updated=False`.\n* Known key, payload equal to the stored payload: nothing at all changes -- not\n  the revision, not `updated_at`, not the state, not the file.  Returns\n  `created=False, updated=False`.\n* Known key, payload different: replace the payload, increment `revision` by 1,\n  set `updated_at` to now, force `state` back to `\"pending\"`, clear `owner`,\n  `lease_expires_at` and `result`.  `seq`, `created_at`, `attempts` and\n  `acked_revision` are preserved, so a claimed entry loses its lease and a done\n  entry is reopened while remembering the revision that was last acknowledged.\n  Returns `created=False, updated=True`.\n\nThe returned `SubmitResult.entry` is always a copy.\n\n### `claim(owner, lease_seconds, limit=1) -> list[Entry]`\n\nReads the clock once, then expires every entry whose lease ended at or before\nnow (as in `sweep`), so a just-expired entry is claimable in the same call.\nThen it takes up to `limit` entries whose state is `\"pending\"`, in ascending\n`seq` order, and for each sets `state = \"claimed\"`, `owner`,\n`lease_expires_at = now + lease_seconds`, `updated_at = now`, and increments\n`attempts` by 1.  Returns copies, in ascending `seq` order.\n\n### `ack(key, owner, result=None, *, revision=None) -> Entry`\n\nValidates its arguments, reads the clock once, expires stale leases, then:\nraises `UnknownKeyError` for an unknown key; raises `LeaseError` when the entry\nis not `\"claimed\"` or when `entry.owner != owner`; raises `ConflictError` when\n`revision` is not `None` and differs from the entry's current `revision`, in\nwhich case nothing changes.  Otherwise it sets `state = \"done\"`, stores a deep\ncopy of `result` (or `None`), sets `acked_revision` to the entry's `revision`,\nclears `owner` and `lease_expires_at`, sets `updated_at = now`, and returns a\ncopy.  `attempts` and `revision` are unchanged.\n\n### `release(key, owner) -> Entry`\n\nSame argument validation, clock read, expiry and `UnknownKeyError`/`LeaseError`\nrules as `ack`, but it returns the entry to `\"pending\"`, clears `owner` and\n`lease_expires_at` and sets `updated_at = now`.  `attempts`, `revision`,\n`result` and `acked_revision` are untouched.  Returns a copy.\n\n### `sweep() -> int`\n\nReads the clock once and returns every entry whose lease expired to `\"pending\"`,\nclearing `owner` and `lease_expires_at` and setting `updated_at = now`.\n`attempts`, `revision` and `result` are untouched.  Returns how many entries\nwere expired.\n\n### `get(key) -> Entry | None`, `list_entries(state=None) -> list[Entry]`, `stats() -> dict`\n\n`get` returns a copy, or `None` for an unknown key.  `list_entries` returns\ncopies in ascending `seq` order, filtered by `state` when one is given.  `stats`\nreturns a freshly built dict with exactly the keys `\"pending\"`, `\"claimed\"`,\n`\"done\"` and `\"total\"`.  None of the three ever mutates stored state, so an\nentry whose lease has